  "worked_solution": "Two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability density functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nSince our random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 3y \\\\  & =6xy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $(x,y)\\in [0,1]\\times [1,\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}].$ Therefore, the full expression for $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0.5,Y\\le 1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}6xy\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}6x{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2/3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.5}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{0.5} \\\\  & =(0.5)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
